/* Periodic MIDI-OUT Active Sensing (0xFE) as a link keep-alive.
   Per the MIDI spec a receiver declares the link dead after 300ms of silence following a 0xFE,
   so send every 250ms for margin. Sent only in MIDI mode (DIP-SW3 ON): in latch mode U108 pulls
   the Q103 base to GND and disables the MIDI-OUT driver, so nothing would reach the wire anyway. */
#define MIDI_AS_INTERVAL_MS 250u
static volatile uint32_t  midi_as_timer_ms = MIDI_AS_INTERVAL_MS;
static volatile bool      midi_as_pending  = false;

/* Do not send our own Active Sensing while Thru traffic is flowing.
   poll_midi_out_sensing() writes 0xFE as soon as TXE is free, so unconditionally it would land
   in the middle of a Thru message (between the 2nd and 3rd byte of a CC, say). The MIDI spec
   allows System Real-Time bytes anywhere and obliges receivers to pass them through, but
   **some receivers really do drop such a sequence** (observed with CH34x-based USB-MIDI
   interfaces). To avoid losses downstream, hold our 0xFE back while Thru has just been active.

   Why this is safe: the Active Sensing timeout (300ms) means "declare the link dead if no MIDI
   data at all arrives within 300ms of a 0xFE". While Thru is passing data the downstream timer
   keeps being reset, so suppressing our own 0xFE cannot cause a false disconnect. Upstream 0xFE
   is passed through by Thru as well, so whatever the upstream sends still reaches the downstream.

   THRU_IDLE_GUARD_MS is set well above the inter-byte gap within a message (320us at 31250bps).
   5ms exceeds a whole 3-byte message (960us) and still leaves room for jitter. */
#define THRU_IDLE_GUARD_MS 5u
static volatile uint32_t  thru_idle_ms   = 0;  /* Guard time remaining since the last Thru byte [ms] */
static volatile uint16_t  midi_as_tx     = 0;  /* Number of 0xFE actually transmitted (MSTAT) */
static volatile uint16_t  midi_as_hold   = 0;  /* Number of 0xFE delayed by the guard (MSTAT) */
static volatile bool      midi_as_held   = false; /* Whether the current pending 0xFE is already counted */

